Issues.Body:
[Issue #71631](https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/71631) | [Triage notes](https://github.com/Flarette/angular/blob/bdf5f73a2ca87d891ee8b806ff23664606fb2a7f/packages/forms/signals/triage/debounce-abort-signal.md) | [Repro specs](https://github.com/Flarette/angular/blob/bdf5f73a2ca87d891ee8b806ff23664606fb2a7f/packages/forms/signals/test/node/debounce_abort.spec.ts)

## Why the change

A custom debouncer's abort listener runs late and inside a `linkedSignal` computation, so a listener that touches a signal throws NG0600 or drops the user's value; this draft adds failing repro specs and asks you to choose the fix before I write it.

## Special things to note

- This is a triage draft with no fix. 7 specs fail on purpose, so CI is red: the 6 new repros and
  the existing direct-write spec in `debounce.spec.ts`, which now actually resolves its debounce.
- After you answer the questions below, I'll push the fix to this branch and delete `packages/forms/signals/triage/`.
- The built-in debouncers, `debounce(path, ms)` and `debounce(path, 'blur')`, aren't affected.

## Change outline

What the branch adds:

```text
packages/forms/signals/
├── test/node/api/debounce.spec.ts     # existing direct-write spec now resolves; fails until the fix
├── test/node/debounce_abort.spec.ts   # 6 failing repros, 1 passing control spec
└── triage/debounce-abort-signal.md    # observed output, root cause, measured fix options
```

Where the abort runs today, in the normal type, wait, blur flow:

```text
user types "ng"
  debounceSync()
    pendingSync.set(controller)
debouncer resolves
  sync()                                  value = "ng"; pendingSync still holds controller
user blurs
  markAsTouched()
    flushSync()
      pendingSync()                       linkedSignal recomputes now, with tracking on
        computation(previous = controller)
          controller.abort()              aborts a debounce that already finished
            abort listener                user code, inside a computation
              searching.set(false)        NG0600, caught by dispatchEvent, reported as uncaught
```

### Recommended fix: make `pendingSync` a plain field

Abort explicitly and untracked, and clear the field when a debounce completes
(`src/field/node.ts`):

```diff
-  private readonly pendingSync: WritableSignal<AbortController | undefined> = linkedSignal({
-    source: () => this.value(),
-    computation: (_source, previous) => {
-      previous?.value?.abort();
-      return undefined;
-    },
-  });
+  private pendingSync: AbortController | undefined = undefined;

   private _reset(value?: unknown) {
-    this.pendingSync()?.abort();
+    this.abortPendingSync();

   flushSync() {
-    const pending = this.pendingSync();
-    if (pending && !pending.signal.aborted) {
-      pending.abort();
+    if (this.pendingSync) {
+      this.abortPendingSync();
       this.sync();
     }
   }

+  private abortPendingSync(): void {
+    const pending = this.pendingSync;
+    this.pendingSync = undefined;
+    // Abort listeners are user code, so keep their signal reads and writes out of any reactive
+    // context.
+    untracked(() => pending?.abort());
+  }

   private async debounceSync() {
-    const debouncer = untracked(() => {
-      this.pendingSync()?.abort();
-      return this.nodeState.debouncer();
-    });
+    this.abortPendingSync();
+    const debouncer = untracked(this.nodeState.debouncer);
     if (debouncer) {
       const controller = new AbortController();
       const promise = debouncer(controller.signal);
       if (promise) {
-        this.pendingSync.set(controller);
+        const valueBefore = untracked(this.value);
+        this.pendingSync = controller;
         await promise;
-        if (controller.signal.aborted) {
+        if (controller.signal.aborted || this.structure.isOrphaned()) {
           return; // Do not sync if the debounce was aborted.
         }
+        this.pendingSync = undefined;
+        if (!Object.is(untracked(this.value), valueBefore)) {
+          return; // Do not sync over a value that was set directly during the debounce.
+        }
       }
     }
```

Measured as a temporary patch: the full node target and the full Chromium suite pass, except the
spec for an immediate abort on a direct write (decision 1). It also removes one `linkedSignal` from
every `FieldNode`, debounced or not.

### Alternative: keep the `linkedSignal` and make it safe

```diff
   computation: (_source, previous) => {
-    previous?.value?.abort();
+    untracked(() => previous?.value?.abort());
     return undefined;
   },

   await promise;
-  if (controller.signal.aborted) {
+  if (controller.signal.aborted || this.structure.isOrphaned()) {
     return; // Do not sync if the debounce was aborted.
   }
+  // Reading `pendingSync` aborts `controller` if `value` changed while it waited.
+  if (untracked(this.pendingSync) !== controller) {
+    return;
+  }
+  // The debounce is done, so a later read of `pendingSync` must not abort it.
+  this.pendingSync.set(undefined);
```

Measured with the same result. The diff is smaller, but user listeners still run during a
computation. The orphan check must come first: reading `pendingSync` reads `value`, which throws
NG01904 on a removed array item.

### Design decisions

1. **When the abort fires.** The guide and the `pendingSync` doc comment say it fires when the value
   changes before the debounce resolves. Both fixes keep today's timing for direct model writes: the
   abort fires at the next keystroke, touch, or reset.
   - Recommended: keep the timing and reword the guide.
   - Alternative: abort right away. That needs an `effect` per debounced field, and effects run
     asynchronously, so it still isn't immediate.

   Current guide text (`async-operations.md:472`):

   ```text
   The `abortSignal` fires when the field is touched, or when its value changes before the
   debounce resolves.
   ```

   Proposed guide text:

   ```text
   The `abortSignal` fires when the field is touched or reset, or when a newer control value
   arrives before the debounce resolves. If the model is written directly, it fires at the next
   of those events, or not at all if the debounce resolves first.
   ```

2. **Plain field or `linkedSignal`.** Recommended: plain field. No user code runs inside a
   computation, and nothing aborts a finished debounce by construction.
3. **A debounce that resolves after a direct model write.** Today its `sync()` writes the current
   value back, which is a no-op for plain fields. On a compat field it calls
   `FormControl.setValue()` and emits `valueChanges` again. The `valueBefore` check skips that
   `sync()`. One flow changes with the plain field: touching after a direct write now reaches
   `sync()` in `flushSync()`, where today the recompute returns `undefined` and skips it. The same
   check would keep that flow as it is today; the measured patch has it only in `debounceSync()`.

   The existing spec "should be ignored if value is directly set before it resolves" already states
   this contract, but it awaited `resolve` without calling it, so it never reached the late resolve.
   This PR fixes the spec and checks that the late debounce doesn't write the model:

   ```text
   debounce by function should be ignored if value is directly set before it resolves
       value.set() calls from the late debounce:
           Unexpected $[0] = [ '2000 N Shoreline Blvd' ] in array.
   ```

   Both measured fixes skip that `sync()`, so they make it pass.

### Questions for maintainers

1. Is the current timing acceptable if the guide is reworded, or should a direct model write abort
   the debounce right away?
2. Can `pendingSync` become a plain field?
3. Should a debounce that resolves after a direct model write skip its `sync()`, as the existing
   spec's name says? If so, should `flushSync()` skip it too?
4. Should abort listeners be able to read and write signals freely? Both fixes assume yes and run
   them in `untracked()`.

